Found while implementing karr #48 (measured on the post-#48 tree):

ArrayRef[Bool] accepts an undef ELEMENT (Types::Standard::Bool includes
undef in its constraint), the attribute stores it as undef -- but the
TO_JSON truthiness map (lib/IO/K8s/Role/Resource.pm:135) emits it as
false:

  bools => [1, undef, 0]   -->   {"bools":[true,false,false]}

Same silent-invented-false class of problem as karr #48, one level down:
the attribute-level fix (undef stays unset, field omitted) does not apply
inside an array, where "omit" is not an option -- the positions would
shift. The honest choices are JSON null (if the field is nullable
upstream) or dying with field + element index (consistent with the karr
#42 messages). Note _normalize_bool now deliberately returns undef via
explicit `return undef` so the array coercer does NOT collapse the
element; the wrong turn happens later, in TO_JSON.

Also worth recording in the same decision: required Bool attributes (7
attrs in 6 classes, e.g. Core::V1::ContainerStatus ready) accept explicit
undef locally because Bool includes undef; after #48 the field is omitted
and the API server rejects the missing required field (before #48 it was
silently sent as false). That is the intended fail-at-server contract, but
if a local fail-closed is ever wanted, it lands in the same few lines.

t/53_bool_normalization.t deliberately does NOT assert the current
array-element behaviour either way, so this ticket owns the decision.

Lane: io-k8s-worker, then io-k8s-test-writer for t/53.

Fixed: TO_JSON is_array_of_bool branch dies on an undef element naming class, field and index (karr #42 style). Element still passes the constructor; refusal at the wire boundary. Coercer untouched. Tests: t/53_bool_normalization.t.
